%T A380137 17,24,15
%N A380137 The conjectured maximum multiplicative persistence for bases b >= 2.
%C A380137 Data from the T. Lamont-Smith article (see the Links section), Chapter 4, Multi-digit numbers, Table 5.
%C A380137 From _Jwalin Bhatt_, Oct 02 2026: (Start)
%C A380137 The terms a(24) through a(39) are supported by Sascha Kurz's table "Behaviour of p_max(b)" (see Links) along with some values updated.
%C A380137 The conjectured values for 32,33,35,36 and 39 are 12,17,16,13 and 19 respectively.
%C A380137 The smallest numbers which achieve a multiplicative persistence of a(n) are given in the linked table. (End)
%H A380137 Jwalin Bhatt, <a href="/A380137/a380137_2.txt">Tables of smallest numbers with given multiplicative persistence</a>
%H A380137 Sascha Kurz, <a href="https://www.mathe2.uni-bayreuth.de/sascha/oeis/persistence/persistence_e.html">Persistence in different bases</a>, 2001.
%H A380137 T. Lamont-Smith, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL24/Lamont/lamont5.html">Multiplicative Persistence and Absolute Multiplicative Persistence</a>, Journal of Integer Sequences, Vol. 24 (2021), Article 21.6.7.
%e A380137 For n = 277777788888899 (the least number with the multiplicative persistence 11) in the base b = 10: 277777788888899 -> 4996238671872 -> 438939648 -> 4478976 -> 338688 -> 27648 -> 2688 -> 768 -> 336 -> 54 -> 20 -> 0. The upper bound of the multiplicative persistence for the base 10 is conjectured to be 11.
